            "doc": "\nThe valid options for the Strategy Type of an operation.\n\nAttributes:\n    AdaptiveClearing2D: A 2D strategy that creates a roughing operation that uses a more optimized toolpath that avoids abrupt direction changes.\n    Pocket2D: A 2D strategy that creates a roughing operation that uses toolpaths parallel to selected geometry.\n    Face: A 2D strategy that produces quick part facing to prepare raw stock for machining.\n    Contour2D: A 2D strategy that creates toolpaths based on a 2D contour.  Contours can be open or closed and can be on\n        different Z-levels, but each contour is flat (2D).\n    Slot: A 2D strategy that mills a slot by following the center line of the slot.\n    Trace: A 2D strategy that machines along contours with varying Z values and with, or without,\n        left and right side compensation.\n    Thread: A 2D strategy for thread milling cylindrical pockets and islands.\n    Bore: A 2D strategy for milling cylindrical pockets and islands by selecting the cylindrical geometry directly.\n    Circular: A 2D strategy for milling cylindrical pockets and islands.\n    Engrave: A 2D strategy that machines along contours with V-shaped chamfered walls.\n    AdaptiveClearing: A 3D roughing strategy for clearing large quantities of material effectively.\n    PocketClearing: A 3D conventional roughing strategy for clearing large quantities of material effectively.\n    Parallel: A 3D finishing strategy.  The passes are parallel in the XY-plane and follow the surface in the Z-direction.\n    Contour: A 3D strategy for finishing steep walls, but can be used for semi-finish and finish machining on the more\n        vertical areas of a part.\n    Ramp: A 3D finishing strategy intended for steep areas similar to the contour strategy.\n        However, this strategy ramps down walls rather than machining them with a constant Z, as contour does.\n    Horizontal: A 3D strategy that automatically detects all the flat areas of the part